  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Robbie Ray shut down the Angels over six scoreless innings. The Giants broke the game open in the third and fourth, scoring nine runs on eleven hits while leaving eight runners on base in missed-opportunity innings that didn't matter once the lead reached nine. Ray's shutout allowed the offense room to coast. This wasn't a comeback or a survival, just a team that controlled the game early and never let it tighten.",
    "what_this_means": "The real story is the margin itself. Nine runs is the kind of lead that erases pressure, and against a middling Angels lineup, it was always going to hold. But the Giants didn't need that cushion because they didn't need to be precise. They didn't trail at any point, took the lead in the third and never gave it back, and converted just enough runners in scoring position to make the early aggression count. Devers drove in four. Lee added three more. The pitching was clean, the bullpen didn't implode, and the result was the kind of flat, resolved game that looks like a team executing rather than surviving. Two wins in a row doesn't rewrite the season, but it does break the noise for a moment. The inconsistency didn't vanish today. It just paused.",
    "what_to_watch": "Jos\u00e9 Soriano takes the ball for the Angels tomorrow with a 3.43 ERA. The Giants will see him again in the same spot they found success today. Early at-bats, runners in scoring position, and a chance to build a lead before the Angels settle in.",
